   "type": "answer",
   "content": "No. The VCF Operations Demand – Usage gap is reserved capacity, not unmet CPU demand / contention.\n\nMechanism. vCenter `cpu.demand` is a sum-of-consumers metric: the ideal VM demand with no contention, reservations excluded. `vsphere/technology/cpu/metrics/host/demand`\nVCF Operations `CPU|Demand` follows the capacity model: per running VM the higher of its demand and its CPU reservation, summed, plus the kernel’s System Resource Reservation. Usage is the same actual use in both tools. The host is quiet with few VMs, so VCF Operations Demand is flat ~2.9 GHz while Usage is ~324-484 MHz. vCenter Demand is ~200 MHz average, i.e. the running VMs’ actual demand.\n\nReason. ESXi CPU Reserved Capacity is the VM reservations only, capped at base clock × cores, and it excludes the kernel reservation. `vsphere/technology/cpu/metrics/host/reserved-capacity` VCF Operations CPU|Demand includes both VM reservations – the higher of demand/reservation per VM – and the kernel reservation that Reserved Capacity excludes. That is why VCF Operations Demand is far above vCenter Demand and above Usage with no contention. A CPU reservation only matters under contention; unused reserved CPU is used by other VMs.\n\nWhat to check next. ESXi Performance % for sun05-esxi47 and the VM contention sub-metrics it aggregates – Worst peak vCPU Ready, Worst peak vCPU Co-stop on the host’s VMs at 20 s peaks – plus Core Utilization and Thread Utilization for capacity headroom, and the host System Resource Reservation to explain the ~2.9 GHz flat VCF Operations demand.",
